    "description": "Medieval scholars connected with Iran developed influential approaches to algebra, astronomical calculation, and trigonometry within networks extending beyond modern national borders. Al-Khwarizmi, Omar Khayyam, and Nasir al-Din al-Tusi illustrate different relationships between mathematical reasoning, observation, and patronage. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["History of science", "Astronomy", "Mathematics", "Algebra", "Observatories"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T07:28:53Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T07:28:53Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Astronomy and Mathematics in Iranian History | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore medieval algebra, Khayyam’s calendar work, and al-Tusi’s astronomy, with attention to geography, patronage, and historical uncertainty."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "knowledge-across-borders",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Knowledge across borders",
        "content": "The history of mathematics associated with Iran cannot be contained within the country's present borders. Al-Khwarizmi worked in Baghdad; Omar Khayyam's career connected Nishapur, Samarkand, and Isfahan; Nasir al-Din al-Tusi worked at Maragheh. These settings belong to different political and geographical histories. Reading their work together reveals an interconnected scholarly world rather than a sequence of isolated national achievements. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nBiography also requires caution. Al-Khwarizmi's name suggests a connection with Khwarazm, south of the Aral Sea, but the surviving evidence does not securely establish his birthplace. His contribution is better explained through his writings than through confident claims about an uncertain origin. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "algebra-as-method",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Algebra as a practical method",
        "content": "Al-Khwarizmi's algebra addressed problems involving inheritance, commerce, land measurement, and other practical calculations. Its equations were expressed in words rather than modern symbolic notation. He organized linear and quadratic problems into standard forms and supplied procedures and geometrical explanations for solving them. [s1]\n\nThe word algebra comes from al-jabr in the title of this work; algorithm developed from a Latin rendering of his name. These linguistic inheritances are useful clues to the transmission of mathematical knowledge, but they should not obscure the particular problems his book was designed to solve. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "khayyam",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Khayyam: equations and the calendar",
        "content": "Omar Khayyam is widely remembered as a poet, but his mathematical work deserves attention on its own terms. His algebra classified cubic equations and used intersections of conic sections—curves such as circles, parabolas, and hyperbolas—to construct solutions. This was a geometrical treatment, distinct from the later algebraic formulas familiar from modern textbooks. [s2]\n\nAt Isfahan, support from the Seljuq ruler Malik-Shah enabled astronomical research. Khayyam participated in the calendar reform of 1079 and in compiling astronomical tables. Calendar making connected observation with the practical task of organizing time. His career also shows the vulnerability of research to court politics: the deaths of Malik-Shah and his minister Nizam al-Mulk in 1092 disrupted the support on which the observatory depended. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "maragheh",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Maragheh: observation and mathematical models",
        "content": "Under Mongol patronage, al-Tusi helped develop the observatory at Maragheh in northwestern Iran. Its library and scholarly activities extended beyond astronomy. His Ilkhanic Tables provided material for calculating planetary positions, while his theoretical writings reconsidered the geometrical machinery used to describe celestial motion. [s3]\n\nThe Tusi couple combines circular motions to produce motion along a straight line. It addressed problems within mathematical astronomy; it was not itself a proposal that Earth orbits the Sun. Similar constructions in Copernicus have prompted debate about transmission, and the inspected account records disagreement about the route of influence. [s3]\n\nAl-Tusi also presented plane and spherical trigonometry systematically. Taken together, these examples show several kinds of scientific work: organizing procedures, constructing geometrical solutions, observing the sky, and revising models. Understanding those differences is more revealing than treating every achievement as a single race toward modern science. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
