    "name": "History of Communication Technologies",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/history/history-of-science-and-technology/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's communication history includes courier networks, organized postal services, electric telegraphy, broadcasting, and digital networks. These technologies changed the speed and reach of communication while raising recurring questions about access, infrastructure, and control. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Communications", "Postal history", "Telegraph", "Radio", "Internet history"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T07:28:53Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T07:28:53Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "History of Communication Technologies in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Trace selected developments in Iranian postal services, telegraphy, broadcasting, and Internet measurement, with clear limits on current coverage."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "moving-messages",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Moving messages across distance",
        "content": "Communication infrastructure changes more than the time a message takes to arrive. It also changes who can send information, which places are connected, and which organizations stand between sender and recipient. Iran's postal and telecommunications history developed alongside transport routes and the institutions that financed and operated them. [s1]\n\nIn the nineteenth century, merchants, travelers, and diplomatic couriers carried correspondence through overlapping arrangements. A historical survey dates regular postal connections between Tehran and major provincial cities to around 1850. British, Russian, and French diplomatic routes also connected Iran with destinations abroad. These arrangements should not be confused with universal household access: an organized route between cities says little by itself about the service available in every settlement. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "telegraph",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Electric messages and the Qajar state",
        "content": "Electric telegraphy separated long-distance message transmission from the movement of a messenger. Its introduction involved experiments, teaching, political support, and construction. Soli Shahvar's study identifies Mirza Malkom Khan, a reformer and teacher, as a central figure in the early experiments at Dar al-Fonun, Tehran's polytechnic college. [s2]\n\nIn 1858, Malkom Khan worked with an Austrian instructor, students, and the school's superintendent to connect rooms at the college. Subsequent local connections reached Golestan Palace and the royal garden at Lalehzar. These short urban links demonstrated the technology before the expansion of longer routes. [s2]\n\nThe connection between Tehran and Tabriz gave the central government a new means of rapid communication with a major provincial center. International expansion, including the Indo-European line completed in 1865, placed Iranian routes within a much wider system. Domestic administration and international strategic interests therefore formed part of the same infrastructure history. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "broadcasting",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From addressed messages to broadcasting",
        "content": "Radio changed the relationship between sender and audience. Instead of addressing a letter or telegram to a particular recipient, a station could transmit a program to dispersed listeners. Radio Iran was formally inaugurated in Tehran on 24 April 1940. The later creation of National Iranian Radio and Television in 1971 brought broadcasting under a combined organization. These institutional milestones establish changes in organization, rather than proving equal reception or equal influence throughout the country. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measuring-digital-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Digital access requires more than a connection",
        "content": "Internet history introduces a further distinction: being connected does not necessarily mean being able to communicate reliably. In a 2013 working paper, researcher Collin Anderson used volunteer network-performance measurements to investigate possible throttling in Iran. Throttling means reducing the rate at which data can move, rather than necessarily disconnecting users altogether. [s3]\n\nThe study compared performance across time and networks, looking for patterns consistent with imposed restrictions. Its limitations are essential to the interpretation: volunteer tests are not a representative household survey, and poor performance alone does not establish deliberate censorship. Congestion, routing problems, and other technical conditions must also be considered. [s3]\n\nThis provides a useful way to read the broader history. A postal route, a telegraph line, a radio transmitter, and an Internet connection each establish a different kind of capacity. Assessing their social significance requires separate evidence about actual reach, reliability, use, and restrictions. The historical milestones here do not establish the condition of Iranian communications in 2026. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
